# Summary of changes for run 09b50d16-d014-47f9-a029-becdd898eb13
I wrote and committed all five deliverables for the "ramified contribution is negligible" result. Two checks could not be run here: there is no LaTeX installation, so `RESEARCH_PAPER.tex` was never compiled, and matplotlib is not installed, so the two plot scripts were never run.

None of the prose documents mentions proof assistants, formal-proof names or file paths.

**The result being packaged.** Take a sample of \(N\) points with \(r\) ramified ones among them. Including those \(r\) points changes the empirical residue-to-type mutual information by at most \(\frac{r}{N}\bigl(3\log_2 N + \frac{2}{\ln 2}\bigr)\) bits, for any pair of read-outs. Other results from the source work:
- the exact decomposition identity behind the bound;
- an example reaching \(\frac{r}{N}\log_2 N\), so the \(\log N / N\) rate cannot be improved;
- a guarantee of at most 0.002 bits for two ramified points once \(N \ge 2^{16}\);
- the effect tends to 0 as \(N\) grows, uniformly over all read-outs;
- the same bound relative to the ideal Galois-group channel in the Chebotarev fibre-product model.

**What was produced:**
- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,000 words): a popular-science account starting from the \(x^2-3\) anomaly (\(+0.002\) bits). It explains the fibre log-sum, why a few extra points are cheap, the main theorem, the sharpness example, a data table and the Galois picture.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 3,650 words): abstract, definitions, every lemma and theorem stated in full with a proof sketch, a comparison with data, algorithms, discussion and future work.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`**: an amsart version of the same paper with numbered theorem environments. Since it could not be compiled, I checked only that braces and `\begin`/`\end` pairs balance, and renamed two custom macros to avoid clashes.
- **`demo.py`**: plain Python, runs in about one second. It reproduces the experiment: at 9,592 primes, adding 2 and 3 moves the channel by \(+0.0026\) bits, against a bound of 0.0089. At every sample size tried the gap is about 0.3 of the bound. It also tested the bound on 3,000 random and adversarial channels with no violation; the largest gap was 0.36 of the bound. Finally it checks the sharpness example.
- **`PACKAGE.json`**, which contains:
  - all the documents above;
  - three algorithms with pseudocode and code: computing the channel from fibre log-sums, an exclusion certificate (for two ramified points and a 0.002-bit tolerance it gives a threshold of 49,688 points), and the sharpness example;
  - two plot scripts;
  - two interactive browser widgets: a live view of the \(x^2-3\) gap against the bound with an "adversary mode", and a playground for adding extra points to fibres. Their JavaScript passed a syntax check, but I did not open them in a browser;
  - a guided page layout using collapsible sections;
  - the full source of the three proof files and their paths;
  - the future directions.

**Choices to check:**
- The domain is "Bridges", because the concept's domain, Probability, is not on the allowed list.
- The future directions were lightly edited so that code names became plain-language result names. The source text broke off in the middle of direction 4, so that item is kept as a marked stub.
- The paper and article say the true constant is about 1, not the proved 3. That is supported only by the numerical data and is stated as a conjecture, not a result.

I did not rebuild the proof files here; this phase only packaged existing work.
